"label":"whole","marks":1,"review_status":"reviewed","subtopic_ids":["E.2"],"depends_on":[],"context_ids":[],"display_text":null,"assets":[{"path":"cross-board-assets/vc23-17-0.webp","dpi":144,"crop_pdf_points_top_left":[0,0,508,98],"source_pdf_page":11,"source_crop":[45,34.35811996459961,580,156.79226684570312],"sha256":"0e53fd7cb32641e9d6afee4f3fef29f08954a1de492c02116cdef14056c58591"}],"answer":{"text":"Correct answer: B.","letter":"B","source_id":"vcaa-a638ff1d9de645a9","section":"Section A, answer table","evidence":"17 | B | 9 | 78 | 6 | 6 | 0 | Electrons exhibit wave properties related to their velocities.","source":{"id":"vcaa-a638ff1d9de645a9","board":"vcaa","course":"VCE-Physics","year":2023,"session":"November","paper":"written","sha256":"228e3e3e2caa985aefca08dab1cd12d85a257a0c0cdd00197e82bfa92c602162","source_url":"https://www.vcaa.vic.edu.au/sites/default/files/Documents/exams/physics/2023/2023physics%20report.docx","local_path":"papers/vcaa/VCE-Physics/2023/2023physics-report--228e3e3e2c.docx","level":"external","qualification":"VCE-Physics","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025","paper_label":"written"}},"given_text":null}],"contexts":[]},{"id":"cross-ca24m4-written-7ci","question_number":"7ci","question_type":"written","ib_level":"HL","source":{"course":"9702","level":"external","year":2024,"session":"March","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","paper":"42","paper_label":"42","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025"},"parts":[{"label":"whole","marks":null,"review_status":"reviewed","subtopic_ids":["E.2"],"depends_on":[],"context_ids":[],"display_text":"Define the threshold wavelength for photoelectric emission from a metal surface.","assets":[],"answer":{"text":"It is the longest wavelength of incident electromagnetic radiation that can cause photoelectrons to be emitted from that surface.","source_id":"cambridge-a2643bb4c378f550","evidence":{"question":"7ci","pages":[11],"review":"Worked answer checked against the source marking guidance; wording adapted for independent IB practice."},"source":{"id":"cambridge-a2643bb4c378f550","board":"cambridge","course":"9702","year":2024,"session":"March","paper":"42","sha256":"ea108d62044d9875829c5d40370d45642d78f217f68b9f48a5b8a81d7787b05c","source_url":"https://pastpapers.papacambridge.com/directories/CAIE/CAIE-pastpapers/upload/9702_m24_ms_42.pdf","local_path":"papers/cambridge/9702/2024/9702_m24_ms_42--ea108d6204.pdf","level":"external","qualification":"9702","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025","paper_label":"42"}},"given_text":null}],"contexts":[]},{"id":"cross-ca24m4-written-7cii","question_number":"7cii","question_type":"written","ib_level":"HL","source":{"course":"9702","level":"external","year":2024,"session":"March","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","paper":"42","paper_label":"42","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025"},"parts":[{"label":"whole","marks":null,"review_status":"reviewed","subtopic_ids":["E.2"],"depends_on":[],"context_ids":[],"display_text":"The work functions of tungsten, magnesium and potassium are 4.49 eV, 3.68 eV and 2.26 eV respectively. Identify which has the longest threshold wavelength and calculate that wavelength. Use h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and 1 eV = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.","assets":[],"answer":{"text":"Potassium has the smallest work function, hence the longest threshold wavelength. λ₀ = hc/Φ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(3.00 × 10⁸)/(2.26 × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 5.50 × 10⁻⁷ m.","source_id":"cambridge-a2643bb4c378f550","evidence":{"question":"7cii","pages":[11],"review":"Worked answer checked against the source marking guidance; wording adapted for independent IB practice."},"source":{"id":"cambridge-a2643bb4c378f550","board":"cambridge","course":"9702","year":2024,"session":"March","paper":"42","sha256":"ea108d62044d9875829c5d40370d45642d78f217f68b9f48a5b8a81d7787b05c","source_url":"https://pastpapers.papacambridge.com/directories/CAIE/CAIE-pastpapers/upload/9702_m24_ms_42.pdf","local_path":"papers/cambridge/9702/2024/9702_m24_ms_42--ea108d6204.pdf","level":"external","qualification":"9702","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025","paper_label":"42"}},"given_text":null}],"contexts":[]},{"id":"cross-ap24p2-written-1a","question_number":"1a","question_type":"written","ib_level":"HL","source":{"course":"Physics-2","level":"external","year":2024,"session":"May","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","paper":"FRQ","paper_label":"FRQ","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025"},"parts":[{"label":"whole","marks":null,"review_status":"reviewed","subtopic_ids":["E.2"],"depends_on":[],"context_ids":[],"display_text":"A photoelectric experiment gives these minimum de Broglie wavelengths for emitted electrons:\nLight frequency fA: metal 1, 6.9 × 10⁻¹⁰ m; metal 2, 9.4 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.\nLight frequency fB: no electrons from either metal.\nLight frequency fC: metal 1, 5.3 × 10⁻¹⁰ m; metal 2, 6.3 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.\n\nRank fA, fB and fC from lowest to highest. Explain using photon energy and electron wavelength.","assets":[],"answer":{"text":"fB < fA < fC. No emission at fB means its photon energy is below threshold. For the same metal, fC produces electrons with a shorter minimum wavelength, hence greater momentum and maximum kinetic energy than fA. Since hf = Φ + Kmax, fC is greater.","source_id":"ap-70c3b0898aaf42d1","evidence":{"question":"1a","pages":[2],"review":"Worked answer checked against the source marking guidance; wording adapted for independent IB practice."},"source":{"id":"ap-70c3b0898aaf42d1","board":"ap","course":"Physics-2","year":2024,"session":"May","paper":"FRQ","sha256":"4802c810bb75bc6b0618fec0654d69e4c29e1162c58c0e187be6f8e8d70c420a","source_url":"https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2026/02/4199-ap24-sg-physics-2.pdf","local_path":"papers/ap/Physics-2/2024/ap24-sg-physics-2--4802c810bb.pdf","level":"external","qualification":"Physics-2","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025","paper_label":"FRQ"}},"given_text":null}],"contexts":[]},{"id":"cross-ap24p2-written-1b","question_number":"1b","question_type":"written","ib_level":"HL","source":{"course":"Physics-2","level":"external","year":2024,"session":"May","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","paper":"FRQ","paper_label":"FRQ","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025"},"parts":[{"label":"whole","marks":null,"review_status":"reviewed","subtopic_ids":["E.2"],"depends_on":[],"context_ids":[],"display_text":"The minimum de Broglie wavelength of electrons emitted from a metal is 6.9 × 10⁻¹⁰ m. Calculate their maximum kinetic energy using non-relativistic mechanics. Use h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and me = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg.","assets":[],"answer":{"text":"p = h/λ and K = p²/(2me), so Kmax = h²/(2meλ²) = 5.1 × 10⁻¹⁹ J (about 3.2 eV).","source_id":"ap-70c3b0898aaf42d1","evidence":{"question":"1b","pages":[3],"review":"Worked answer checked against the source marking guidance; wording adapted for independent IB practice."},"source":{"id":"ap-70c3b0898aaf42d1","board":"ap","course":"Physics-2","year":2024,"session":"May","paper":"FRQ","sha256":"4802c810bb75bc6b0618fec0654d69e4c29e1162c58c0e187be6f8e8d70c420a","source_url":"https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2026/02/4199-ap24-sg-physics-2.pdf","local_path":"papers/ap/Physics-2/2024/ap24-sg-physics-2--4802c810bb.pdf","level":"external","qualification":"Physics-2","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025","paper_label":"FRQ"}},"given_text":null}],"contexts":[]},{"id":"cross-ap24p2-written-1c","question_number":"1c","question_type":"written","ib_level":"HL","source":{"course":"Physics-2","level":"external","year":2024,"session":"May","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","paper":"FRQ","paper_label":"FRQ","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025"},"parts":[{"label":"whole","marks":null,"review_status":"reviewed","subtopic_ids":["E.2"],"depends_on":[],"context_ids":[],"display_text":"A photoelectric experiment gives these minimum de Broglie wavelengths for emitted electrons:\nLight frequency fA: metal 1, 6.9 × 10⁻¹⁰ m; metal 2, 9.4 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.\nLight frequency fB: no electrons from either metal.\nLight frequency fC: metal 1, 5.3 × 10⁻¹⁰ m; metal 2, 6.3 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.\n\nCompare the work functions of the two metals, with a reason based on the data.","assets":[],"answer":{"text":"Metal 1 has the smaller work function. At the same incident frequency its emitted electrons have the shorter minimum wavelength, hence greater maximum kinetic energy. With the same hf, Φ = hf − Kmax is smaller.","source_id":"ap-70c3b0898aaf42d1","evidence":{"question":"1c","pages":[4],"review":"Worked answer checked against the source marking guidance; wording adapted for independent IB practice."},"source":{"id":"ap-70c3b0898aaf42d1","board":"ap","course":"Physics-2","year":2024,"session":"May","paper":"FRQ","sha256":"4802c810bb75bc6b0618fec0654d69e4c29e1162c58c0e187be6f8e8d70c420a","source_url":"https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2026/02/4199-ap24-sg-physics-2.pdf","local_path":"papers/ap/Physics-2/2024/ap24-sg-physics-2--4802c810bb.pdf","level":"external","qualification":"Physics-2","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025","paper_label":"FRQ"}},"given_text":null}],"contexts":[]}],"guidance":{"entries":{"fep-b2fd7afe31aa819a-q35:whole":{"hint":"Connect the particle’s wavelength to its momentum, rather than treating it as a photon.","notes":["The de Broglie wavelength is λ = h/p. For a non-relativistic particle, p = mv or √(2mE_k).","If accelerated from rest through a voltage magnitude V, E_k = |q|V. Use this only in the non-relativistic regime; E = hc/λ is a photon relation, not a general massive-particle kinetic-energy equation."]},"fep-1c98cb85f0bf7153-q33:whole":{"hint":"Consider the energy carried by one photon and the energy needed to release one electron.","notes":["Einstein’s photoelectric equation is hf = φ + E_k,max. At threshold, hf₀ = φ; a stopping potential gives E_k,max = eV_stop.","Above threshold, intensity mainly changes the number of emitted electrons; frequency changes the maximum kinetic energy. Below threshold, greater intensity cannot compensate for insufficient photon energy."]},"fep-1c98cb85f0bf7153-q36:whole":{"hint":"The shortest possible wavelength comes from the largest energy that one electron can give to one photon.","notes":["An electron accelerated through potential V gains energy eV. If it becomes one X-ray photon, hc/λ_min = eV.","Most collisions transfer only part of the electron energy, producing a continuous spectrum. Characteristic lines come from atomic transitions in the target."]},"fep-2055ded62a83a7c9-q40:whole":{"hint":"Check both the energy needed to create rest mass and the momentum balance.","notes":["Creating an electron–positron pair needs at least 2m_ec², with additional energy supplying kinetic energy and recoil. A nearby nucleus can absorb recoil momentum.","An initially stationary electron–positron pair has zero total momentum. Its annihilation photons must balance momentum as well as carrying the released energy."]},"fep-4702f1ae6785e44e-q37:whole":{"hint":"Consider the energy carried by one photon and the energy needed to release one electron.","notes":["Einstein’s photoelectric equation is hf = φ + E_k,max. At threshold, hf₀ = φ; a stopping potential gives E_k,max = eV_stop.","Above threshold, intensity mainly changes the number of emitted electrons; frequency changes the maximum kinetic energy. Below threshold, greater intensity cannot compensate for insufficient photon energy."]},"fep-4702f1ae6785e44e-q39:whole":{"hint":"Connect the particle’s wavelength to its momentum, rather than treating it as a photon.","notes":["The de Broglie wavelength is λ = h/p. For a non-relativistic particle, p = mv or √(2mE_k).","If accelerated from rest through a voltage magnitude V, E_k = |q|V. Use this only in the non-relativistic regime; E = hc/λ is a photon relation, not a general massive-particle kinetic-energy equation."]},"fep-d5113d0c3f9c0cb5-q40:whole":{"hint":"Consider the energy carried by one photon and the energy needed to release one electron.","notes":["Einstein’s photoelectric equation is hf = φ + E_k,max. At threshold, hf₀ = φ; a stopping potential gives E_k,max = eV_stop.","Above threshold, intensity mainly changes the number of emitted electrons; frequency changes the maximum kinetic energy. Below threshold, greater intensity cannot compensate for insufficient photon energy."]},"cross-ed24u2-4:whole":{"hint":"Ask which observation each model can explain, rather than assuming one model explains every experiment.","notes":["Interference and diffraction show wave behaviour; the photoelectric effect shows quantized energy transfer. Use the model suited to the observation.","Wave–particle duality does not mean every classical wave property and every classical particle property are observed simultaneously in one measurement."],"method":"duality","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-ed24u2-6:whole":{"hint":"Consider the energy carried by one photon and the energy needed to release one electron.","notes":["Einstein’s photoelectric equation is hf = φ + E_k,max. At threshold, hf₀ = φ; a stopping potential gives E_k,max = eV_stop.","Above threshold, intensity mainly changes the number of emitted electrons; frequency changes the maximum kinetic energy. Below threshold, greater intensity cannot compensate for insufficient photon energy."],"method":"photoelectric","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-ed24u2-10:whole":{"hint":"Consider the energy carried by one photon and the energy needed to release one electron.","notes":["Einstein’s photoelectric equation is hf = φ + E_k,max. At threshold, hf₀ = φ; a stopping potential gives E_k,max = eV_stop.","Above threshold, intensity mainly changes the number of emitted electrons; frequency changes the maximum kinetic energy. Below threshold, greater intensity cannot compensate for insufficient photon energy."],"method":"photoelectric","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-aq24p1-9:whole":{"hint":"Consider the energy carried by one photon and the energy needed to release one electron.","notes":["Einstein’s photoelectric equation is hf = φ + E_k,max. At threshold, hf₀ = φ; a stopping potential gives E_k,max = eV_stop.","Above threshold, intensity mainly changes the number of emitted electrons; frequency changes the maximum kinetic energy. Below threshold, greater intensity cannot compensate for insufficient photon energy."],"method":"photoelectric","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-aq24p1-10:whole":{"hint":"Consider the energy carried by one photon and the energy needed to release one electron.","notes":["Einstein’s photoelectric equation is hf = φ + E_k,max. At threshold, hf₀ = φ; a stopping potential gives E_k,max = eV_stop.","Above threshold, intensity mainly changes the number of emitted electrons; frequency changes the maximum kinetic energy. Below threshold, greater intensity cannot compensate for insufficient photon energy."],"method":"photoelectric","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-aq24p1-12:whole":{"hint":"Connect the particle’s wavelength to its momentum, rather than treating it as a photon.","notes":["The de Broglie wavelength is λ = h/p. For a non-relativistic particle, p = mv or √(2mE_k).","If accelerated from rest through a voltage magnitude V, E_k = |q|V. Use this only in the non-relativistic regime; E = hc/λ is a photon relation, not a general massive-particle kinetic-energy equation."],"method":"de-broglie","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-vc23-17:whole":{"hint":"Ask which observation each model can explain, rather than assuming one model explains every experiment.","notes":["Interference and diffraction show wave behaviour; the photoelectric effect shows quantized energy transfer. Use the model suited to the observation.","Wave–particle duality does not mean every classical wave property and every classical particle property are observed simultaneously in one measurement."],"method":"duality","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-ca24m4-written-7ci:whole":{"hint":"Longer-wavelength photons carry less energy. Which limiting wavelength just supplies the work function?","notes":["Threshold frequency is a minimum, whereas threshold wavelength is a maximum, because E = hf = hc/λ."],"method":"photoelectric","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-ca24m4-written-7cii:whole":{"hint":"Compare the work functions before calculating three separate wavelengths.","notes":["At threshold the maximum photoelectron kinetic energy is zero. Convert the work function to joules if h is in J s."],"method":"photoelectric","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-ap24p2-written-1a:whole":{"hint":"Compare trials using the same metal so that its work function stays fixed.","notes":["Minimum electron wavelength corresponds to maximum electron momentum and kinetic energy. Do not confuse the wavelength of the emitted electron with the incident light wavelength."],"method":"photoelectric","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-ap24p2-written-1b:whole":{"hint":"Find momentum from the matter wavelength before finding kinetic energy.","notes":["E = hc/λ applies to photons, not massive electrons. For non-relativistic electrons combine λ = h/p with K = p²/(2m)."],"method":"de-broglie","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-ap24p2-written-1c:whole":{"hint":"Keep incident photon energy fixed when comparing the metals.","notes":["Compare either the fA pair or the fC pair. A larger maximum electron energy leaves less of the photon energy needed to overcome the work function."],"method":"photoelectric","review_status":"reviewed"}}},"answers":{"entries":{}},"presentation":{"practice/parts/fep-b2fd7afe31aa819a-q35-whole-735fa64039cd579b.webp":{"crop":[0,2,1094,362],"size":[1094,433],"masks":[[7,5,33,29]]},"practice/parts/fep-1c98cb85f0bf7153-q33-whole-990a603eddb29763.webp":{"crop":[0,3,1094,835],"size":[1094,1403],"masks":[[7,5,33,29]]},"practice/parts/fep-1c98cb85f0bf7153-q36-whole-fbb1954fb11be422.webp":{"crop":[0,3,1072,260],"size":[1072,341],"masks":[[7,5,33,29]]},"practice/parts/fep-2055ded62a83a7c9-q40-whole-042d9b6aedc7cc2b.webp":{"crop":[0,7,1072,281],"size":[1072,310],"masks":[[7,5,33,36]]},"practice/parts/fep-4702f1ae6785e44e-q37-whole-e9e1f89992030d93.webp":{"crop":[0,3,1094,678],"size":[1094,1403],"masks":[[7,4,33,30]]},"practice/parts/fep-4702f1ae6785e44e-q39-whole-2c5d0faac941641a.webp":{"crop":[0,3,1094,326],"size":[1094,483],"masks":[[7,4,33,30]]},"practice/parts/fep-d5113d0c3f9c0cb5-q40-whole-3cd98ec12257f0a2.webp":{"crop":[0,3,1094,396],"size":[1094,766],"masks":[[7,5,33,29]]}}}